invariant: INV-26
file: quic_aes_soft.c
catches: proof/prove-one.sh aes
reason: the key expansion's loop runs one word past round_keys, so the
  last four stores land outside the round key array and inside whatever
  the caller placed after it on its stack frame. INV-26 is what admits a
  table-driven AES into this tree, and aes_harness is what holds
  the admitted code to memory safety at its real bound, so this is the
  bug that harness exists to catch. The loop moved here from aes.c
  when the AES axis split the cipher out; aes_hw.c carries the same
  loop, and no proof reads it (docs/quic.md, "What the AES axis
  proves"). docs/proofs.md requires planting it once and landing it.
  Each run is a proof of seconds, but the catch line names
  proof/prove-one.sh, so this belongs to the nightly's
  test-invariants-proof-backed job.

--- old
    for (size_t i = AES_128_KEY; i < (size_t)AES_ROUND_KEYS * AES_BLOCK; i += 4) {
--- new
    for (size_t i = AES_128_KEY; i <= (size_t)AES_ROUND_KEYS * AES_BLOCK; i += 4) {
